
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This directory contains the pointing, efficiency function, observer ephemeris
and detections of the N26 HST heliostack: the Napier et al. (2026, Planet.
Sci. J. 7, 117; arXiv:2604.16731) reanalysis of the Bernstein et al. (2004,
AJ 128, 1364) HST GO-9433 ACS/WFC F606W field as a single 15-day heliostack.
It is a single-epoch survey (one stack), not a multi-epoch AND.

Photometry is HST ACS F606W in STMAG, filter letter t (not r, not w):

    t = STMAG_F606W = r_AB + 0.3

so colour.toml has "t-g" = "r-g" + 0.3 in every spectral group. The 0.3 mag
ST->AB conversion is the one adopted for this survey; the Napier vs Bernstein
zeropoint offset on the two objects in both catalogs is ~0.2 mag.

The content of this directory is:

N26
   \---------- README.n26 (this file)   Describes the content of the directory
    \--------- pointings.list           One rectangle: the 6-tile 400" x 600"
     \                                  ACS/WFC mosaic at 14:07:53.33
      \                                 -11:21:38, JD 2452672.8585 (stack
       \                                midpoint), fill factor 1
        \----- N26.eff                  Detection efficiency, filter= t
         \---- HST.csv                  JPL Horizons barycentric ICRF vectors
          \                             of HST (-48), 2003-Feb-02 06:00-14:00
           \                            TDB at 1 minute steps
            \- HST.jpl_batch            The Horizons batch request that
                                        produced HST.csv
   \---------- N26.csv                  The five detected objects (CSV)
    \--------- N26.toml                 survey_debias configuration

Where the values come from:

- Efficiency (N26.eff): Napier Eq. 20 logistic
      eta = eta0 / (1 + exp((m - m50)/sigma)),
      eta0 = 1.0, m50 = 29.21 +/- 0.03 STMAG, sigma = 0.11 +/- 0.02,
  written as the OSSSSim single tanh A/2 (1 - tanh((m - M0)/w)) with
  A = eta0, M0 = m50 = 29.21, w = 2 sigma = 0.22. The tracking fraction is 1
  (the characterized sample is the stack itself) with the same knee.
- mag_lim = 29.80: Napier's implant faint limit is 29.7 STMAG (Sec. III.1);
  0.1 mag of headroom keeps mag_lim off the efficiency knee.
- mag_error: the OSSOS photometric-scatter template shifted so the knee is
  at 29.2 STMAG. It is not fitted to the HST photometry.
- rate_cut = 0.05-6.4 "/hr, isotropic: the cone of the OSSSSim HST example
  (examples/Surveys/HST/HST.eff). Napier searches barycentric CCKBO
  trajectories and corrects HST parallax in the stack; the simulator's
  rate is the 2 h motion from HST.csv at the 15-day midpoint.
- Pointing: field centre and mosaic size of the Bernstein et al. 2004
  GO-9433 field; the epoch is the 15-day stack midpoint.
- Detections (N26.csv): the survey is the union of the Bernstein et al.
  single-exposure ("bright") search and the Napier heliostack ("faint")
  search. The heliostack masks every >4 sigma pixel in each exposure, so it
  cannot find objects visible in a single 400 s exposure (m <~ 27.6); those
  are covered by the B04 bright search, which is 100% complete there.
  - 2003 BF91, BH91 (B04 discoveries) and 2003 ABCD, WXYZ (new in Napier):
    m_stmag, i, d_bary, ifree from Napier Tables 1 and 2; a and e are a_min
    and e_min.
  - 2003 BG91: a B04 discovery from the bright search, detected in both the
    discovery and recovery epochs and recovered at Keck. Napier saw it but
    did not measure it. m_stmag = B04 <m> 26.95 shifted by -0.19, the mean
    Napier - B04 offset on BF91 and BH91; a, e, i from JPL SBDB (92-day arc);
    d_bary from Horizons at the stack midpoint; ifree from the survey_debias
    Laplace-plane approximation with Omega = 176.
  - 2000 FV53 is excluded: it is the object the field was placed on.
- Effective area: pointings.list is the nominal 400" x 600" footprint at the
  stack midpoint with fill 1. Two known approximations:
  - The mosaic is aligned to the ecliptic (B04 Sec. II.1), i.e. at PA 19.7
    deg in ICRS, but rect is axis-aligned in RA/Dec. Area is unchanged; only
    the corners differ. A poly entry would represent it exactly.
  - Over the 15-day span a CCKBO moves ~40-45" in ecliptic longitude and
    +/-15-20" in latitude (Horizons tracks of BF91 and BG91), so objects
    near the outer edges get fewer exposures in the full stack. A crude
    model of the 2x3 mosaic and visit pattern gives 86% of the footprint
    with full coverage, and an area-weighted efficiency 1.5% below Napier's
    eta at m < 28, 5% at 28.8, and 9% at m50. We assume Napier's efficiency
    counts every implanted source that landed on a pixel, so eta already
    includes this loss and the nominal footprint is the right area (to be
    confirmed with K. Napier). B04's 0.019 deg^2 counts an object as in the
    field if it is imaged in >= 3 visits of the discovery epoch, so it does
    not apply to the 15-day stack.
- rate_cut: at the stack midpoint the field is at its stationary point and
  the simulated 2 h rate is 0.06-0.24 "/hr for cold orbits at 35-70 au, so
  0.05-6.4 "/hr imposes no outer distance limit (see N26.eff).
- Napier's search grid is restricted to a in [42.4, 47.4] au, e in [0, 0.2],
  ecliptic i in [0, 5] deg, r in [38, 51] au. The faint-search efficiency
  applies only inside that range; rate_cut does not encode it.

These files were moved here from hst-tno-followup/characterization/ and
hst-tno-followup/data/n26_detections.csv. The .eff file there was in r_AB
(filter= r, every magnitude 0.3 brighter than here); it is now in native
STMAG so it matches the m_stmag detections.

See CFEPS_g/README.formats for the format of a survey "block".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
